  "activity": {
    "purpose": "The object of the company is, in Belgium and abroad, the operation of restaurants, bars and catering businesses, the preparation and sale of meals and drinks for consumption on and off the premises, and all transactions directly or indirectly related to it.",
    "offer": "The Copper Pot is a neighbourhood restaurant with 60 seats in the centre of Antwerp, serving seasonal Belgian cooking.\n\n- Lunch from Tuesday to Friday, with a set lunch menu.\n- Dinner from Tuesday to Saturday, à la carte.\n- Drinks: Belgian beers, a short wine list and soft drinks.",
    "business_model": "Guests pay at the table, by card or in cash. A lunch costs on average {{stream.lunch.price}} excluding VAT and a dinner {{stream.dinner.price}}; guests spend on average another {{stream.drinks.price}} on drinks. Food is charged at 12% VAT, drinks at 21%.\n\nFood costs are about 30% of the food price, drinks about 27% of the drinks price. Suppliers are paid at 30 days.",
    "market": "The restaurant is located in a busy street with offices and residents nearby. Lunch targets office workers, dinner targets residents and visitors. Several restaurants in the area serve international cuisine; few offer Belgian seasonal cooking at this price level.",
    "outlook": "After the plan's horizon, the founders consider adding a small terrace and private dining for groups."
  },
  "assumptions": {
    "years": [
      "The first financial year runs for 16 months, from September 2027 to December 2028. The premises are fitted out in September and October 2027; the restaurant opens on 1 November 2027. It starts with about 20 guests per lunch and 25 per dinner, rising to about 32 and 42 in the second half of 2028, with 17 lunch days and 22 dinner days a month and a two-week closure in August. The head chef starts in October to prepare the opening; the cook, a part-time kitchen assistant, a waiter and flexi-job waiters start in November, and a part-time waiter joins in March 2028. Revenue amounts to {{revenue.1}}.",
      "In the second financial year, the restaurant serves about 35 guests per lunch and 45 per dinner. A second cook is hired in March 2029 and the manager's remuneration increases. Prices rise by 3%. Revenue amounts to {{revenue.2}} and the operating result to {{operating_result.2}}.",
      "In the third financial year, guest numbers grow to about 38 per lunch and 48 per dinner, and prices rise by another 3%. Revenue amounts to {{revenue.3}} and the net result to {{net_result.3}}."
    ],
    "profitability": "The gross margin is {{gm_pct.1}} of revenue in the first financial year and {{gm_pct.3}} in the third; it is measured after food and drinks purchases and after services and other goods, including rent and the manager's remuneration. Staff costs are the largest cost item: {{staff_cost.2}} in the second financial year. The operating result is {{operating_result.1}} in the first financial year, which includes the two months of fit-out without revenue, and {{operating_result.3}} in the third. The lowest cash position is {{lowest_cash}} in {{lowest_cash_month}}.",
    "risks": "- Fewer guests than expected: the founders will close on Tuesdays and reduce the flexi-job hours.\n- Higher food and energy prices: the menu prices are reviewed every six months.\n- Delays in the fit-out: the opening campaign is postponed, and the bank loan's grace period covers the first six months."
  },
